{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"agent-ready-repo-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Agent-ready repository track","description":"Prepares a repository for coding agents in four gated steps, from a timed setup audit and verified instruction files to least-privilege guardrails and a trial task that tests them.","category":"meta","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","build","verify"],"role":["tech-lead","software-engineer","devops-engineer"],"requires":["repo-read","file-write","shell"],"inputs":["repo"],"output":["report","docs","config"],"risk":"runs-commands","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["coding-agents","agent-instructions","developer-experience","least-privilege"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-agents-md","audit-agent-permissions","exclude-files-from-agent-context","write-agent-environment-setup","design-coding-agent-hooks"],"personas":["careful-coding-agent"]},"args":[{"name":"goal","description":"How the team wants to use agents in this repository, for example supervised sessions on features or unattended bug fixes in the cloud.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"trial_task","description":"A small real task from the backlog to test the setup in the last step. Leave empty to pick one from open issues or TODOs.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"audit","file":"steps/01-audit.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"agent-ready/01-audit.md"},{"id":"instructions","file":"steps/02-instructions.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"guardrails","file":"steps/03-guardrails.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"trial","file":"steps/04-trial.md","stage":"verify","gate":"none","artifact":"agent-ready/04-report.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Makes this repository ready for coding agents used for: {{goal}}. An agent is only as good as the feedback the repository gives it: a setup that works from scratch, checks that run in seconds, instructions that are true, and permissions that match the job. This track measures those first, fixes them in small reviewed steps, and ends by running a real task to see where an agent still stumbles.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Record only what you ran and saw, with the command and its real result.\n- Every command written into an instruction file must have been run successfully in this track.\n- Never read or print secret values; refer to sensitive files by path.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.","steps":[{"id":"audit","file":"steps/01-audit.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"agent-ready/01-audit.md","text":"# Step 1: Audit\n\n1. Follow the repository's own setup docs from a clean state as far as the environment allows. Time install, build, the full test suite and lint. Note every step that needed a guess, a manual fix or a credential.\n2. Find the fastest feedback commands: a single test file or test name, type check, lint on one file. Time them. Note if none exists.\n3. Read existing agent instruction files and contributor docs. Mark lines that are stale (a command that failed or no longer exists), generic, or conflicting.\n4. List sensitive and noisy paths by name: environment files, keys, data exports, generated code, vendored code, large fixtures.\n5. Check how changes are verified in CI and whether the local commands match.\n\nWrite the artifact: Setup log (step, command, result, time), Feedback commands (command, time, scope), Instruction file issues, Sensitive and noisy paths, Gaps ranked by how much they would hurt an agent working on: {{goal}}. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"instructions","file":"steps/02-instructions.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Instructions\n\n1. Write or update the root AGENTS.md (or the file the team's tools read): setup, the fast feedback commands from step 1, the full check that must pass before a change is done, layout with one example file per pattern, and boundaries (paths never to edit, actions that need approval).\n2. Use only commands that ran successfully in step 1. Where a gap remains (no fast test command, setup needs a credential), state it plainly instead of papering over it.\n3. Remove or fix every stale, generic or conflicting line found in step 1, and list each removal with its reason.\n4. Add a short nested instruction file only for an area with at least two rules that differ from the root.\n5. If other tools' instruction files exist, make them point to the canonical file instead of repeating it.\n\nShow the diff of instruction files and the list of removals. Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"guardrails","file":"steps/03-guardrails.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Guardrails\n\n1. Propose the agent permission configuration for {{goal}}: allow the build, test and lint commands from step 2 without approval; require approval for installing dependencies, network access beyond package registries, pushing and anything outside the repository; deny reading the sensitive paths from step 1 and editing the agent's own configuration.\n2. Add an ignore configuration for the noisy paths, keeping readable any generated file the code imports. State that ignore files do not protect secrets from shell access.\n3. If a sensitive file holds a real secret in the working tree, do not move or rotate it yourself; list it for the team with the recommendation.\n4. Where the audit showed slow or missing feedback, propose the smallest fix, for example a script that runs tests for changed files or a fast lint target, and add it only if approved.\n5. Optionally propose one or two hooks with clear value (format after edit, block commands touching protected paths). Keep each fast.\n\nShow the configuration files and any new scripts as diffs, each with one line on why. Mark keys you are not sure of \"check against the tool's documentation\". Stop and wait for approval."},{"id":"trial","file":"steps/04-trial.md","stage":"verify","gate":"none","artifact":"agent-ready/04-report.md","text":"# Step 4: Trial and report\n\n1. {{#trial_task}}Take the trial task: {{trial_task}}. {{/trial_task}}If no trial task was given, pick a small, well-defined open issue or TODO and say which.\n2. Work it as a fresh agent would: read only the instruction files first, then do the task using the documented commands. Note every point where the instructions were missing, wrong or ignored, and every permission prompt or denial.\n3. Do not merge or push. Leave the change on a branch or as an uncommitted diff for the team.\n4. Fix instruction gaps the trial exposed, if they are small and clearly within the approved scope.\n\nWrite the report:\n\n## Before and after\nSetup time, fastest feedback command and its time, full check time, instruction file size.\n\n## Trial\nTask, result, checks run with real output, friction points and what was changed because of them.\n\n## Open items\nTable: item (secrets to rotate, missing fast tests, credentials needed for setup), why it matters, suggested owner.\n\n## Next\nWhich agent use the repository is now ready for, and what must change before more autonomy."}]}
